{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 argues decentralization caps correlated-failure blast radius via operational-plane diversity, but its own counterconditions concede the exact defeating evidence the problem asks it to distinguish: if all decentralized nodes share a common upstream dependency or a common vulnerable library, the 'diversity of operational plane' does not prevent correlated failure — it merely makes the correlation invisible until the shared cause is exercised. The problem explicitly demands evidence that distinguishes truly independent failures from merely apparently-uncorrelated ones. SRC_001 provides no such distinguishing evidence; its mechanism (different OS/language/operators) is exactly the kind of superficial diversity that can mask a latent common-cause path. Thus the argument fails the problem's second requirement: it asserts independence from operational diversity without identifying the evidence that would falsify that independence.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Decentralized services A–Z run different OSes, languages, and are operated by independent teams (genuine operational-plane diversity per SRC_001), but all link the same vulnerable TLS library. A single bad update to that library is a poison pill that propagates across A–Z simultaneously. Operational-plane diversity did not cap the blast radius; it only hid the shared cause until triggered — the precise undistinguished failure mode the problem asks to identify evidence for."}],"target":"06c4a9146f3cc3babb1d11160503208db6cb2c909cacc645801eae40ac1f3426"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's mechanism claims the decentralized system's 'baseline noise' masks shared-cause failures whereas a large service's 'quiet' state makes correlated failures 'immediately obvious and easier to isolate.' This is asserted without evidence and is directly counterexampled: a single global control-plane 'poison pill' update in a large, internally redundant service can take the entire service down at once, and the quiet state gives no advance warning at all — the correlated failure is not 'immediately obvious' before it occurs, and isolation may be impossible after the service is globally offline. Conversely, the decentralized 'noise' of small independent failures can surface a shared-cause bug in a subset of nodes while the rest survive, making the correlation more detectable, not less. SRC_002 therefore fails to identify the evidence the problem requires for distinguishing true independence from apparent non-correlation; it simply inverts the masking direction without empirical grounding.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Large redundant service with 100x replicas sharing one control plane receives a poison-pill config push. All replicas fail simultaneously; there is no 'quiet' survivable state to observe and no partial-failure signal to isolate. A decentralized network hit by the same shared library bug fails across, say, 30% of nodes that use that library; the surviving 70% and the partial pattern make the shared cause easier to detect. This contradicts SRC_002's claim that noise masks and quiet aids isolation."}],"target":"6db3914d094b3dcafa2d3f9f128263ae7465813ca7bd8ffe70e5966da6aa8148"}]}